// tamil-demo — Tamil script typesetting in texish.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/tamil-demo.script
//
// Tamil is written left to right, so it needs none of the bidirectional
// reordering Hebrew and Arabic do. What it needs is work inside each syllable,
// and Tamil asks for less of that than the scripts to its north — but asks for
// it differently.
//
// A consonant silenced by the virama keeps the virama as a visible dot, the
// pulli, and stands as a complete letter of its own. It does not fold into a
// half-form the way Devanagari's does, and nothing is ever subjoined beneath
// the base as in Telugu. Only two conjuncts survive from Sanskrit, ksha and the
// sacred shrii, each drawn as a single ligature. There is no reph: a
// word-initial ra with a pulli stays an ordinary letter.
//
// The consequence for the shaper is where a pre-base vowel sign lands. Three
// signs are drawn before their consonant, and because a silenced consonant is a
// letter in its own right the sign moves before the consonant it belongs to and
// no further — not to the front of the whole syllable as it would in
// Devanagari.
//
// \font tamil selects the bundled face, in a regular and a bold cut. No \rtl is
// needed — the script is left to right.

\use{document}

\title{The Tamil Script in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Setting a Tamil paragraph}

Each syllable is shaped on its own and the syllables are set left to right. Within a
syllable the vowel signs are placed around the consonant and the pulli is hung above it;
between syllables the line breaker finds its breaks at the interword spaces exactly as for
Latin text.

\medskip

{\font tamil 14 regular
தமிழ் ஒரு அழகான மொழி. இது இடமிருந்து வலமாக எழுதப்படுகிறது. ஒவ்வொரு எழுத்தும் ஒரு
மெய்யெழுத்தும் அதனுடன் சேர்ந்த உயிரெழுத்தும் இணைந்து உருவாகிறது.
}

\section{The twelve vowel signs}

The same consonant carries each of the twelve vowels. Some signs follow the consonant, some
precede it, and three are written as two parts — one before and one after:

\medskip

{\font tamil 16 regular
க கா கி கீ கு கூ கெ கே கை கொ கோ கௌ
}

\section{The pulli silences a consonant}

A consonant with a pulli above it is a letter in its own right, not half of a conjunct. It
keeps its full shape and its dot, and the syllable that follows begins afresh — which is
why a pre-base vowel sign stops at the pulli rather than leaping over it. In
{\font tamil 12 regular க்கே} the vowel belongs to the second \italic{ka}, and is drawn
between the two. Hello, thank you, and school:

\medskip

{\font tamil 16 regular
வணக்கம்   நன்றி   பள்ளி
}

\section{The two surviving conjuncts}

Tamil borrowed only a handful of Sanskrit conjuncts, and the font draws each as a single
ligature. Here the pulli disappears into the drawn form, and a vowel sign on such a
syllable does reach the front, since nothing is left to stand in its way:

\medskip

{\font tamil 16 regular
க்ஷ   ஸ்ரீ   க்ஷே   ஸ்ரீதர்
}

\section{No reph}

The consonant \italic{ra} takes no special form at the head of a syllable. In
{\font tamil 12 regular ர்க} the ra keeps its pulli and stays where it is, where Devanagari
would lift it above the syllable as a reph:

\medskip

{\font tamil 16 regular
ர்க   கார்   பார்வை
}

\section{Independent vowels and the aytham}

A vowel that opens a syllable is written with its own letter, not a sign. The aytham, which
Tamil uses to write sounds it has no letter for, stands alone between them:

\medskip

{\font tamil 16 regular
அ ஆ இ ஈ உ ஊ எ ஏ ஐ ஒ ஓ ஔ ஃ
}

\section{A closing line}

A greeting, set in the bold cut:

\medskip

{\font tamil 18 bold
வணக்கம்! இது தமிழ் எழுத்தில் எழுதப்பட்டுள்ளது.
}
